## Which denominator should drive the routing decision

**Tokens per parallel sentence.** A routing-and-cost decision is a question
about cost per unit of *meaning served*, and a denominator is only admissible
if it holds meaning constant across languages. Of the four, exactly one does.

**Per word does not.** "Word" is a unit of orthography, not of meaning, and its
size varies with morphology. The same {{ n_records }} sentences are
{{ arg.eng_words }} whitespace words in English and {{ arg.kan_words }} in
Kannada — an agglutinative language packs more meaning into one word. So
tokens/word conflates tokenizer behaviour with word formation, and it does so
by a measurable amount: Kannada reads {{ arg.kan_per_word }}× English per word
but {{ arg.kan_per_sentence }}× per sentence. The gap is agglutination being
counted as tokenizer cost.

**Per byte and per grapheme are diagnostics, not decision metrics.** The
per-byte ratio for Hindi is {{ arg.hin_per_byte }}×, but Indic codepoints cost
three UTF-8 bytes to Latin's one — that ratio measures UTF-8's design as much
as the tokenizer's, and it is useful mainly for spotting byte-fallback. The
per-grapheme ratio ({{ arg.hin_per_grapheme }}× for Hindi) is the right unit
*within* a script, but grapheme clustering is not uniform *across* Indic
scripts: the Devanagari conjunct `क्ष` is one cluster while the structurally
identical Kannada conjunct `ಕ್ಕ` is two. A cross-script per-grapheme
comparison does not hold "one perceived character" constant.

**Per parallel sentence does, by construction.** Index *i* is the same
proposition in every language, so the unit is the same object regardless of how
each language spells it. It also has a property none of the others has: because
every line's denominator is exactly one, micro and macro averaging are
algebraically identical — the weighting choice cannot move the number. It is
the only denominator whose value is not a methodological choice.

**And under it, the tokenizer dominates the language.** Holding the denominator
fixed at the parallel sentence and varying only the tokenizer, Hindi reads
{{ arg.hin_per_sentence }}× English under `gpt2`, {{ arg.hin_xlmr }}× under
XLM-R and {{ arg.hin_muril }}× under MuRIL; Bengali reads {{ arg.ben_muril }}×
under MuRIL. The cost premium is a property of the vocabulary, not of the
script — which is what the single number should be measuring, and what a
single-tokenizer measurement cannot see.
